<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="The wedge-dash structure of chloramphenicol." style="width: 493px; height: 128px;" width="493px" height="128px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472940/15e45a39f065d2e1b40834b46436f2881a060435_1ugf?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=493&amp;height=128" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-68 <i>Allenes</i> are compounds with adjacent carbon&ndash;carbon double bonds. Many allenes are chiral, even though they don&rsquo;t contain chirality centers. Mycomycin, for example, a naturally occurring antibiotic isolated from the bacterium <i>Nocardia acidophilus,</i> is chiral and has [<i>&alpha;</i>]<sub>D</sub> = &minus;130. Explain why mycomycin is chiral.</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="The condensed structural formula of mycomycin." style="width: 562px; height: 67px;" width="562px" height="67px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472944/1a1ae28fafe4e8f373ee362f5881073eea5c6667_48na?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=562&amp;height=67" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-69 Long before chiral allenes were known (Problem 5-68), the resolution of 4-methylcyclohexylideneacetic acid into two enantiomers had been carried out. Why is it chiral? What geometric similarity does it have to allenes?</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="The wedge-dash structure of 4-methylcyclohexylideneacetic acid." style="width: 329px; height: 137px;" width="329px" height="137px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472941/2a6976432433ffc75bf8a19a0aa954df21d10635_h2pu?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=329&amp;height=137" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-70 (<i>S</i>)-1-Chloro-2-methylbutane undergoes light-induced reaction with Cl<sub>2</sub> to yield a mixture of products, among which are 1,4-dichloro-2-methylbutane and 1,2-dichloro-2-methylbutane.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Write the reaction, showing the correct stereochemistry of the reactant.</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> One of the two products is optically active, but the other is optically inactive. Which is which?</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-71</p>

<p>How many stereoisomers of 2,4-dibromo-3-chloropentane are there? Draw them, and indicate which are optically active.</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-72</p>

<p>Draw both <i>cis</i>- and <i>trans</i>-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane in their more stable chair conformations.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> How many stereoisomers are there of <i>cis</i>-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane, and how many of <i>trans</i>-1,4-dimethylcyclohexane?</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Are any of the structures chiral?</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> What are the stereochemical relationships among the various stereoisomers of 1,4-dimethylcyclohexane?</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-73</p>

<p>Draw both <i>cis</i>- and <i>trans</i>-1,3-dimethylcyclohexane in their more stable chair conformations.</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> How many stereoisomers are there of <i>cis</i>-1,3-dimethylcyclohexane, and how many of <i>trans</i>-1,3-dimethylcyclohexane?</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Are any of the structures chiral?</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> What are the stereochemical relationships among the various stereoisomers of 1,3-dimethylcyclohexane?</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-74</p>

<p><i>cis</i>-1,2-Dimethylcyclohexane is optically inactive even though it has two chirality centers. Explain.</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-75</p>

<p>Alkyl halides react with hydrosulfide ion (HS<sup>&minus;</sup>) to give a product whose stereochemistry is inverted from that of the reactant. Draw the reaction of (<i>S</i>)-2-bromobutane with HS<sup>&minus;</sup> ion to yield 2-butanethiol, CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH(SH)CH<sub>3</sub>. Is the stereochemistry of the product <i>R</i> or <i>S</i>?</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="The figure shows a reaction where an alkyl bromide reacts with H S anion  to form a thiol compound and a bromide ion." style="width: 314px; height: 136px;" width="314px" height="136px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472942/2ef18963e13dfb37949e991a2e5dce74a8998350_hi2l?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=314&amp;height=136" /></p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-76 Ketones react with sodium acetylide (the sodium salt of acetylene, Na<sup>+&ndash;</sup> : C&equiv;CH) to give alcohols. For example, the reaction of sodium acetylide with 2-butanone yields 3-methyl-1-pentyn-3-ol:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="In a reaction, 2-butanone reacts with Na superscript plus C superscript minus triple bonded to C H in first step and hydronium ion in the second step to form 3-methyl-1-pentyn-3-ol." style="width: 528px; height: 129px;" width="528px" height="129px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472943/a5b997c47951a97fcdcac49c1a7e10cd36144147_0ye7?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=528&amp;height=129" /></p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Is the product chiral? <strong>(b)</strong> Assuming that the reaction takes place with equal likelihood from both Re and Si faces of the carbonyl group, is the product optically active? Explain.</p>

<p><strong>PROBLEM5</strong>-77 Imagine that a reaction similar to that in Problem 5-76 is carried out between sodium acetylide and (<i>R</i>)-2-phenylpropanal to yield 4-phenyl-1-pentyn-3-ol:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center"><img alt="In a reaction, (R)-2-phenylpropanal reacts with Na superscript plus C superscript minus triple bonded to C H in first step and hydronium ion in the second step to form 4-phenyl-1-pentyn-3-ol." style="width: 562px; height: 177px;" width="562px" height="177px" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/472945/2050885015f7d0fac87a7e26d9960c8e6808e0a2_kckl?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=562&amp;height=177" /></p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Is the product chiral?</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Draw both major and minor reaction products, assuming that the reaction takes place preferentially from the Re face of the carbonyl group. Is the product mixture optically active? Explain.</p>
